            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Do You Need a "Creative Portfolio" Instead of Single Creative at Cold Launch?</h2>
<p>TikTok's traffic distribution mechanism means each creative has a very short display window. An video typically gets most of its impressions within 48-72 hours, after which exposure drops sharply. If you only prepare one creative and keep tweaking it, your risk is highly concentrated—either you hit positive ROI, or your entire account fails at cold launch.</p>
<p>The core logic of a creative portfolio is: let the algorithm quickly find the optimal combination among multiple variables. Hook styles, product USPs, usage scenarios, music rhythms—figuring out which combinations work requires sufficient test samples. The era of betting everything on a single creative is over on TikTok Shop.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Many Creatives Do You Actually Need at Cold Launch?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: 20 is the minimum threshold, 30-50 is more reliable.</strong></p>
<p>This isn't a random number. One cross-border team launching a utility app ran only 8 creatives in the first week, resulting in scattered data where none achieved statistical significance. In the second week, they expanded to 30 creatives, found 2 high-performing creatives within 3 days, and successfully completed cold launch.</p>
<p>Of course, it depends on your category. Low-priced fast-moving goods should prepare 40+ creatives, while high-ticket items can be compressed to 20, but the floor should not fall below 15. With too few creatives, the testing cycle gets stretched out, and you might miss the platform's new product traffic window.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Should Your Creative Portfolio Be Structured?</h2>
<p>It's recommended to divide creatives into three layers, each with different testing objectives and budget weights:</p>
<p><strong>Layer 1: Hook Creatives (40%)</strong></p>
<p>The core task is to boost watch time. Within 3 seconds, you must present a question, contrast, or suspense. Examples: "99% of people don't know this organization hack" or "Spent $30 to solve a problem that troubled me for three years." These creatives need high completion rates but can have lower conversion goals—they mainly signal to the algorithm that "users love this."</p>
<p><strong>Layer 2: Trust Creatives (40%)</strong></p>
<p>Show real product usage scenarios with user testimonials or factory footage. These creatives may have lower CTR than hook creatives but higher shopping cart conversion rates. They're solving the user's question of "is this worth buying?"</p>
<p><strong>Layer 3: Conversion Creatives (20%)</strong></p>
<p>Highlight price advantages, limited-time offers, and gift strategies, directly guiding purchase behavior. These creatives have limited creative space but provide the most direct ROI contribution. They're best used after identifying high-performing creatives to quickly replicate templates and scale output.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What's the Right Ratio of UGC vs. Brand Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>During cold launch, UGC should account for no less than 70%.</strong></p>
<p>TikTok users value "authenticity" far more than "polish." One beauty cross-border team ran a comparative test: a $3,000 brand promotional video achieved only 0.3% CTR, while UGC-style product review content with the same budget generated 1.8 ROI.</p>
<p>This doesn't mean brand creatives are worthless—they're suitable for use in the trust creative layer to provide "professional" backing. But the main attack direction must be UGC. Keep the ratio around 3:7, and consider increasing brand content investment after identifying breakout creatives.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Do You Decide Which Creatives to Stop or Scale?</h2>
<p><strong>Two core metrics: 5-second completion rate and shopping cart CTR.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>5-second completion rate &lt; 30%: The opening fails to hook users. Stop immediately, don't linger.</li>
<li>5-second completion rate &gt; 40%, but shopping cart CTR &lt; 1%: The content attracts attention but product USPs aren't clearly communicated. Try re-cutting with different subtitles and voiceovers.</li>
<li>5-second completion rate &gt; 50%, and shopping cart CTR &gt; 1.5%: High-potential creative. Scale up aggressively.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be patient with data. Each creative needs at least 3,000 impressions before drawing conclusions—decisions based on insufficient data are often wrong. Also, don't look at single metrics in isolation; evaluate them in combination.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Should You Pay Attention to in Creative Localization?</h2>
<p>Localization isn't just subtitle translation. Here are the most commonly overlooked pitfalls:</p>
<p><strong>1. USP Translations Need Re-validation</strong></p>
<p>For the same storage product, emphasizing "tidiness and order" performed poorly in Middle East markets—users there cared more about privacy protection. USP priorities can be completely reversed across different markets.</p>
<p><strong>2. Background Music Isn't a Universal Option</strong></p>
<p>One cross-border team used a trending Chinese BGM in the US market with poor results. After switching to American pop music, CTR doubled. Local users have low tolerance for "foreignness."</p>
<p><strong>3. Find the Right Faces for On-Camera Talent</strong></p>
<p>Southeast Asian users have higher acceptance of local faces, while European and American users respond better to "real consumer" imagery. Talent selection strategy should differ by market.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Should Cold Launch Budget Be Allocated?</h2>
<p><strong>Each creative should have a minimum daily budget of $50.</strong></p>
<p>With insufficient budget, data accumulation is too slow for the algorithm to learn effectively. The recommended allocation strategy is the "721 Rule":</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>70% budget</strong>: Test the initial creative pool, evenly distributed across all candidates</li>
<li><strong>20% budget</strong>: Add to creatives that show positive data</li>
<li><strong>10% budget</strong>: Reserve for developing new creatives to keep the pool fresh</li>
</ul>
<p>Also, don't frequently adjust budgets during cold launch. The platform algorithm needs 3-5 days to complete a learning cycle—adjusting too often actually disrupts the model. Review data once a week and make batch adjustments based on the past 7 days' performance.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>The essence of TikTok Shop cold launch is exchanging sufficient creative samples for the algorithm's understanding of your product. Prepare enough creatives, test in layers, use data to eliminate underperformers, and concentrate resources on breakout creatives—this is a proven cold launch formula.</p>
<p>Remember: A creative portfolio isn't built once and done. It needs continuous updates. After finding a breakout creative, quickly replicate the template and develop new testing creatives to keep the portfolio alive. Otherwise, when the breakout creative decays, your account will face the dilemma of a second cold launch.</p>
